      "heading": "Paying at a Western Union counter, or funding Xoom from an account",
      "body": "<p><a href=\"https://www.westernunion.com/us/en/legal/terms-conditions.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer nofollow\">Western Union's US terms</a> say its services are provided through Locations, its website, its mobile app and a telephone money transfer service, and define a Location as a place where you can send and receive money in person: banks, supermarkets, financial institutions, postal offices, kiosks and other retailers. Payment options depend on the country you send from. Besides a bank card, the terms say you may pay with cash at the Location, and the total due must be paid before Western Union processes the transaction. Its <a href=\"https://www.westernunion.com/us/en/send-money.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer nofollow\">send money page</a> also offers a mixed route: start the transfer online, then pay in store.</p><p>Xoom is reached through its websites and mobile apps, and its <a href=\"https://www.xoom.com/user-agreement\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer nofollow\">user agreement</a> names a credit card, debit card, bank account or PayPal balance as ways to pay, adding that not every method is open to every customer at all times. For some transactions Xoom uses what it calls Pay Only When Received: your payment method is not debited until the recipient, or the company receiving a bill payment or reload, gets the funds. Whether a transaction qualifies depends on factors including the destination, payment method, amount, your transaction history and Xoom's own verification system, and you may only learn after authorising it.</p><p>Paying in person also brings a federal record rule into play. Under <a href=\"https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-31/subtitle-B/chapter-X/part-1010/subpart-D/section-1010.410\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">31 CFR 1010.410(e)</a>, a US office or agent of a non-bank financial institution that accepts a transmittal of $3,000 or more must record the sender's name and address, the amount and the payment instructions. If a sender who is not an established customer places the order in person, it must verify their identity before accepting it and note the type and number of the ID, such as a driver's licence. For an order not placed in person, it records the taxpayer identification number and how the sender paid.</p>"
